Well, eventually.  There are earlier Usenet uses,
established in times predating the Net, for wine
making and for piano noodling, but the earliest
surviving file-oriented use seems to belong to one
of the Usenet Elder Gods, Richard Salz, in a comment
in a source code file posted to comp.sources.unix,
which he happened also to moderate:

> * ftpin: recv data, plonk in file.

http://groups.google.com/groups?selm=713%40fig.bbn.com&output=gplain

Since the subsequent users were people who would
very likely have read and used that posting, Salz
may well be "the fountainhead of 'plonk'", but the
usage mutated on him.

The earliest surviving usage as a killfile plop
sound isn't in talk.bizarre, it's in alt.flame,
but considering that the usage belongs to Richard
Sexton, that talk.bizarre/net.bizarre predates
alt.flame, and that the article _references_
talk.bizarre, "*plonk*" may still _originate_ in
t.b; Google's archives that far back are extremely
sparse.

http://groups.google.com/groups?selm=22087%40gryphon.COM&output=gplain

The earliest surviving "*plonk*" _in_ talk.bizarre
belongs to John Woods, but both his and Richard's
usage look like long established habits in the
corresponding two postings.

http://groups.google.com/groups?selm=10904%40frog.UUCP&output=gplain

Happy to muddle the issue still more.

xanthian, who was there at the time, but doesn't
